  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"嗚呼","reading":"ああ","meaning":"Ah!, oh!, or alas!.","partOfSpeech":"Expression","frequencyRank":74,"mnemonic":"Picture 嗚呼 wearing a name tag that says ''aa'', proudly announcing ah!, oh!, or alas! like it just won a tiny game show.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":1,"pitchAccent":"atamadaka","nuance":"Use 嗚呼 as a set expression in common conversation. It carries its meaning as a whole phrase, so it is best learned with short examples and the situation where it naturally appears.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"ああ！","en":"Ah!","furigana":"ああ！"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"嗚","radical":"口","meaning":"weep","reading":"ああ"},{"kanji":"呼","radical":"口","meaning":"call","reading":"よぶ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000002', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"相","reading":"あい","meaning":"Together, mutually, or fellow.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Imagine 相 as a button labeled ''ai''; press it and the room instantly demonstrates together, mutually, or fellow with too much confidence.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":1,"pitchAccent":"atamadaka","nuance":"Use 相 as the basic noun for together, mutually, or fellow. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"同病相憐れむ。","en":"Misery loves company.","furigana":"どうびょうしょうあわれむ。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"相","radical":"目","meaning":"inter-","reading":"あい"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000003', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"愛想","reading":"あいそ","meaning":"Amiability, friendliness, or affability.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Think of 愛想 shouting ''aiso'' from a billboard whenever amiability, friendliness, or affability appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 愛想 as the basic noun for amiability, friendliness, or affability. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"お愛想お願いします。","en":"The check, please.","furigana":"おあいそおねがいします。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"愛","radical":"心","meaning":"love","reading":"いとしい"},{"kanji":"想","radical":"心","meaning":"idea","reading":"おもう"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000004', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"愛憎","reading":"あいぞう","meaning":"Love and hate.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"See 愛憎 on a sticky note stuck to love and hate; the note keeps saying ''aizou'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 愛憎 as the basic noun for love and hate. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"フロイトは、親子間の愛憎関係をエディプス・コンプレックスとして展開しました。","en":"Freud developed the love-hate relationship between parents and child as the Oedipus complex.","furigana":"フロイトは、おやこかんのあいぞうかんけいをエディプス・コンプレックスとしててんかいしました。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"愛","radical":"心","meaning":"love","reading":"いとしい"},{"kanji":"憎","radical":"心","meaning":"hate","reading":"にくむ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000005', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"相対","reading":"あいたい","meaning":"Involving only the parties concerned or tete-a-tete.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 相対 and saying ''aitai'' while acting out involving only the parties concerned or tete-a-tete with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":1,"pitchAccent":"atamadaka","nuance":"Use 相対 as the basic noun for involving only the parties concerned or tete-a-tete. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"時間は相対的な物だ。","en":"Time is relative.","furigana":"じかんはそうたいてきなものだ。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"相","radical":"目","meaning":"inter-","reading":"あい"},{"kanji":"対","radical":"寸","meaning":"vis-a-vis","reading":"あいて"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000006', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"間柄","reading":"あいだがら","meaning":"Relationship, relation, or terms (good, friendly, etc.).","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture 間柄 wearing a name tag that says ''aidagara'', proudly announcing relationship, relation, or terms (good, friendly, etc.) like it just won a tiny game show.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 間柄 as the basic noun for relationship, relation, or terms (good, friendly, etc.). It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"いとこの間柄です。","en":"We are cousins.","furigana":"いとこのあいだがらです。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"間","radical":"門","meaning":"interval","reading":"あいだ"},{"kanji":"柄","radical":"木","meaning":"design","reading":"がら"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000007', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"合間","reading":"あいま","meaning":"Interval, break, or pause.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":4456,"mnemonic":"Imagine 合間 as a button labeled ''aima''; press it and the room instantly demonstrates interval, break, or pause with too much confidence.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 合間 as the basic noun for interval, break, or pause. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"彼は合間に釣りに行った。","en":"He went fishing in between.","furigana":"かれはあいまにつりにいった。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"合","radical":"口","meaning":"fit","reading":"あう"},{"kanji":"間","radical":"門","meaning":"interval","reading":"あいだ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000008', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"敢えて","reading":"あえて","meaning":"Purposely, daringly (doing something), or deliberately.","partOfSpeech":"Adverb","frequencyRank":1936,"mnemonic":"Think of 敢えて shouting ''aete'' from a billboard whenever purposely, daringly (doing something), or deliberately appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":1,"pitchAccent":"atamadaka","nuance":"Use 敢えて to adjust how an action, state, or whole sentence feels. It usually sits near the verb or adjective it modifies and adds timing, degree, manner, or speaker attitude.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"敢えてやってみた。","en":"I took risks.","furigana":"あえてやってみた。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"敢","radical":"攴","meaning":"daring","reading":"あ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-000000000009', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"仰ぐ","reading":"あおぐ","meaning":"To look up (at).","partOfSpeech":"う verb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"See 仰ぐ on a sticky note stuck to look up (at); the note keeps saying ''aogu'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":2,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 仰ぐ for the action or change of look up (at). The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"空を仰ぎ見る。","en":"I looked up at the sky.","furigana":"そらをあおぎみる。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"仰","radical":"人","meaning":"face-up","reading":"あお"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"垢","reading":"あか","meaning":"Dirt, filth, or grime.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 垢 and saying ''aka'' while acting out dirt, filth, or grime with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":2,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 垢 as the basic noun for dirt, filth, or grime. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"チリダニは、人のフケや、アカを好む。","en":"Dust mites love things like dandruff and dead skin cells.","furigana":"チリダニは、ひとのフケや、アカをこのむ。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"垢","radical":"土","meaning":"dirt","reading":"あか"}]}'::jsonb),
