  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003dc', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"嘸","reading":"さぞ","meaning":"Surely, certainly, or no doubt.","partOfSpeech":"Adverb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Think of 嘸 shouting ''sazo'' from a billboard whenever surely, certainly, or no doubt appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":1,"pitchAccent":"atamadaka","nuance":"Use 嘸 to adjust how an action, state, or whole sentence feels. It usually sits near the verb or adjective it modifies and adds timing, degree, manner, or speaker attitude.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"さぞショックだったでしょう。","en":"It must have been a shock to you.","furigana":"さぞショックだったでしょう。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"嘸","radical":"口","meaning":"how","reading":"さぞ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003dd', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"定める","reading":"さだめる","meaning":"To decide or determine.","partOfSpeech":"る verb","frequencyRank":714,"mnemonic":"See 定める on a sticky note stuck to decide or determine; the note keeps saying ''sadameru'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 定める for the action or change of decide or determine. The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"彼女は的にねらいを定めた。","en":"She aimed at the target.","furigana":"かのじょはてきにねらいをさだめた。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"定","radical":"宀","meaning":"determine","reading":"さだ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003de', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"錯覚","reading":"さっかく","meaning":"Illusion or hallucination.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":4558,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 錯覚 and saying ''sakkaku'' while acting out illusion or hallucination with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 錯覚 as the basic noun for illusion or hallucination. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"それは錯覚です。","en":"It''s an illusion.","furigana":"それはさっかくです。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"錯","radical":"金","meaning":"confused","reading":"サク"},{"kanji":"覚","radical":"見","meaning":"memorize","reading":"おぼえる"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003df', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"察する","reading":"さっする","meaning":"To guess, sense, or presume.","partOfSpeech":"Irregular verb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture 察する wearing a name tag that says ''sassuru'', proudly announcing guess, sense, or presume like it just won a tiny game show.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 察する for the action or change of guess, sense, or presume. The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"あいつの察しの良さは超能力と言っていい。","en":"His guess was so good it could be called ESP.","furigana":"あいつのさっしのよさはちょうのうりょくといっていい。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"察","radical":"宀","meaning":"guess","reading":"さっ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003e0', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"颯と","reading":"さっと","meaning":"Quickly (esp. actions).","partOfSpeech":"Adverb","frequencyRank":3820,"mnemonic":"Imagine 颯と as a button labeled ''satto''; press it and the room instantly demonstrates quickly (esp. actions) with too much confidence.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":null,"pitchAccent":null,"nuance":"Use 颯と to adjust how an action, state, or whole sentence feels. It usually sits near the verb or adjective it modifies and adds timing, degree, manner, or speaker attitude.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"さっとシャワー浴びてくる。","en":"I''m going to take a quick shower.","furigana":"さっとシャワーあびてくる。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"颯","radical":"風","meaning":"sudden","reading":"さっ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003e1', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"冊","reading":"さつ","meaning":"Counter for books.","partOfSpeech":"Counter","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Think of 冊 shouting ''satsu'' from a billboard whenever counter for books appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":null,"pitchAccent":null,"nuance":"Use 冊 when counting or referring to an amount in its usual category. It works with numbers and quantity questions, so the surrounding number often changes the pronunciation pattern.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"本は何冊持ってるの？","en":"How many books do you have?","furigana":"ほんはなんさつもってるの？"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"冊","radical":"冂","meaning":"tome","reading":"さつ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003e2', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"殺人","reading":"さつじん","meaning":"Murder, homicide, or manslaughter.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":4400,"mnemonic":"See 殺人 on a sticky note stuck to murder, homicide, or manslaughter; the note keeps saying ''satsujin'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 殺人 as the basic noun for murder, homicide, or manslaughter. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"殺人はばれるもの。","en":"Murder will out.","furigana":"さつじんはばれるもの。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"殺","radical":"殳","meaning":"kill","reading":"ころす"},{"kanji":"人","radical":"人","meaning":"person","reading":"ひと"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003e3', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"悟る","reading":"さとる","meaning":"To sense, perceive, or notice.","partOfSpeech":"う verb","frequencyRank":4633,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 悟る and saying ''satoru'' while acting out sense, perceive, or notice with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 悟る for the action or change of sense, perceive, or notice. The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"勝てないって悟った。","en":"I realized I couldn''t win.","furigana":"かてないってさとった。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"悟","radical":"心","meaning":"enlightenment","reading":"さと"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003e4', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"裁く","reading":"さばく","meaning":"To judge, try, or sit in judgement (on).","partOfSpeech":"う verb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture 裁く wearing a name tag that says ''sabaku'', proudly announcing judge, try, or sit in judgement (on) like it just won a tiny game show.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":2,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 裁く for the action or change of judge, try, or sit in judgement (on). The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"御子を信じるものは裁かれない。","en":"Whoever believes in him is not condemned.","furigana":"みこをしんじるものはさばかれない。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"裁","radical":"衣","meaning":"tailor","reading":"さば"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003e5', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"然程","reading":"さほど","meaning":"(not) so, (not) particularly, or (not) very.","partOfSpeech":"Adverb","frequencyRank":3992,"mnemonic":"Imagine 然程 as a button labeled ''sahodo''; press it and the room instantly demonstrates (not) so, (not) particularly, or (not) very with too much confidence.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":null,"pitchAccent":null,"nuance":"Use 然程 to adjust how an action, state, or whole sentence feels. It usually sits near the verb or adjective it modifies and adds timing, degree, manner, or speaker attitude.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"流れはさほど速くない。","en":"The stream is not very swift.","furigana":"ながれはさほどはやくない。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"然","radical":"火","meaning":"sort of thing","reading":"しか"},{"kanji":"程","radical":"禾","meaning":"extent","reading":"ほど"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003e6', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"サボる","reading":"サボる","meaning":"To be truant, slack off, or play hooky.","partOfSpeech":"う verb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Think of サボる shouting ''saboru'' from a billboard whenever be truant, slack off, or play hooky appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":null,"pitchAccent":null,"nuance":"Use サボる for the action or change of be truant, slack off, or play hooky. The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"よくサボってた。","en":"I often played truant.","furigana":"よくサボってた。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003e7', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"様","reading":"さま","meaning":"Mr, Mrs, or Miss.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":4025,"mnemonic":"See 様 on a sticky note stuck to mr, Mrs, or Miss; the note keeps saying ''sama'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":2,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 様 as the basic noun for mr, Mrs, or Miss. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"ご苦労様でした。","en":"Thank you for your trouble.","furigana":"ごくろうさまでした。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"様","radical":"木","meaning":"Esq.","reading":"さま"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000003e8', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"寒気","reading":"さむけ","meaning":"Chill, the shivers, or shivering fit.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 寒気 and saying ''samuke'' while acting out chill, the shivers, or shivering fit with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 寒気 as the basic noun for chill, the shivers, or shivering fit. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"寒気がします。","en":"I have a chill.","furigana":"さむけがします。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"寒","radical":"宀","meaning":"cold","reading":"さむい"},{"kanji":"気","radical":"气","meaning":"spirit","reading":"いき"}]}'::jsonb)
