  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007b8', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"情け深い","reading":"なさけぶかい","meaning":"Tender-hearted, compassionate, or charitable.","partOfSpeech":"い adjective","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture 情け深い wearing a name tag that says ''nasakebukai'', proudly announcing tender-hearted, compassionate, or charitable like it just won a tiny game show.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":5,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 情け深い to describe something as tender-hearted, compassionate, or charitable. It attaches to simple descriptions and noun phrases, so it is most useful when you want to state a visible quality, condition, feeling, or character.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"これは情け深いです。","en":"This is tender-hearted, compassionate, or charitable.","furigana":"これはなさけぶかいです。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"情","radical":"心","meaning":"feelings","reading":"なさ"},{"kanji":"深","radical":"水","meaning":"deep","reading":"ぶか"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007b9', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"詰る","reading":"なじる","meaning":"To rebuke, scold, or tell off.","partOfSpeech":"う verb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Imagine 詰る as a button labeled ''najiru''; press it and the room instantly demonstrates rebuke, scold, or tell off with too much confidence.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":2,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 詰る for the action or change of rebuke, scold, or tell off. The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"よく詰ることがあります。","en":"There are times when someone may rebuke, scold, or tell off.","furigana":"よくなじることがあります。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"詰","radical":"言","meaning":"packed","reading":"なじ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007ba', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"名高い","reading":"なだかい","meaning":"Famous, noted, or renowned.","partOfSpeech":"い adjective","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Think of 名高い shouting ''nadakai'' from a billboard whenever famous, noted, or renowned appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 名高い to describe something as famous, noted, or renowned. It attaches to simple descriptions and noun phrases, so it is most useful when you want to state a visible quality, condition, feeling, or character.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"彼女は歌手として名高い。","en":"She is famous as a singer.","furigana":"かのじょはかしゅとしてなだかい。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"名","radical":"口","meaning":"name","reading":"な"},{"kanji":"高","radical":"高","meaning":"tall","reading":"たかい"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007bb', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"雪崩","reading":"なだれ","meaning":"Avalanche or snowslide.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"See 雪崩 on a sticky note stuck to avalanche or snowslide; the note keeps saying ''nadare'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 雪崩 as the basic noun for avalanche or snowslide. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"雪崩で道が塞がっていた。","en":"A snowslide obstructed the road.","furigana":"なだれでみちがふさがっていた。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"雪","radical":"雨","meaning":"snow","reading":"ゆき"},{"kanji":"崩","radical":"山","meaning":"crumble","reading":"くずれる"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007bc', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"懐く","reading":"なつく","meaning":"To become attached (to), take (to), or become affectionate (with).","partOfSpeech":"う verb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 懐く and saying ''natsuku'' while acting out become attached (to), take (to), or become affectionate (with) with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":2,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 懐く for the action or change of become attached (to), take (to), or become affectionate (with). The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"よく懐くことがあります。","en":"There are times when someone may become attached (to), take (to), or become affectionate (with).","furigana":"よくなつくことがあります。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"懐","radical":"心","meaning":"feelings","reading":"なつ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007bd', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"名付ける","reading":"なづける","meaning":"To name, call, or christen.","partOfSpeech":"る verb","frequencyRank":3772,"mnemonic":"Picture 名付ける wearing a name tag that says ''nazukeru'', proudly announcing name, call, or christen like it just won a tiny game show.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 名付ける for the action or change of name, call, or christen. The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"娘にメアリーと名付けたい。","en":"I want to name my daughter Mary.","furigana":"むすめにメアリーとなづけたい。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"名","radical":"口","meaning":"name","reading":"な"},{"kanji":"付","radical":"人","meaning":"adhere","reading":"つける"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007be', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"何気ない","reading":"なにげない","meaning":"Casual, unconcerned, or nonchalant.","partOfSpeech":"い adjective","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Imagine 何気ない as a button labeled ''nanigenai''; press it and the room instantly demonstrates casual, unconcerned, or nonchalant with too much confidence.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":null,"pitchAccent":null,"nuance":"Use 何気ない to describe something as casual, unconcerned, or nonchalant. It attaches to simple descriptions and noun phrases, so it is most useful when you want to state a visible quality, condition, feeling, or character.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"私は何気なくその雑誌を見た。","en":"I took a casual look at the magazine.","furigana":"わたしはなにげなくそのざっしをみた。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"何","radical":"人","meaning":"what","reading":"なに"},{"kanji":"気","radical":"气","meaning":"spirit","reading":"いき"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007bf', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"何卒","reading":"なにとぞ","meaning":"Please, kindly, or I beg of you.","partOfSpeech":"Adverb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Think of 何卒 shouting ''nanitozo'' from a billboard whenever please, kindly, or I beg of you appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 何卒 to adjust how an action, state, or whole sentence feels. It usually sits near the verb or adjective it modifies and adds timing, degree, manner, or speaker attitude.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"浅学非才の私ではありますが、何とぞ皆様のお力を賜りたく、ここにお願い申し上げる次第です。","en":"I may not have much to offer in the way of learning or ability, but I want to do whatever I can for us all and humbly ask for your favor.","furigana":"せんがくひさいのわたしではありますが、なにとぞみなさまのおちからをたまわりたく、ここにおねがいもうしあげるしだいです。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"何","radical":"人","meaning":"what","reading":"なに"},{"kanji":"卒","radical":"十","meaning":"graduate","reading":"そっする"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c0', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"何より","reading":"なにより","meaning":"Above anything else, above all, or more than anything.","partOfSpeech":"Adverb","frequencyRank":2551,"mnemonic":"See 何より on a sticky note stuck to above anything else, above all, or more than anything; the note keeps saying ''naniyori'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":null,"pitchAccent":null,"nuance":"Use 何より to adjust how an action, state, or whole sentence feels. It usually sits near the verb or adjective it modifies and adds timing, degree, manner, or speaker attitude.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"可愛い。そして何より品がいい。","en":"It''s cute, and more importantly, of good quality.","furigana":"かわいい。そしてなによりひんがいい。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"何","radical":"人","meaning":"what","reading":"なに"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c1', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"名札","reading":"なふだ","meaning":"Name plate, name tag, or label.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 名札 and saying ''nafuda'' while acting out name plate, name tag, or label with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 名札 as the basic noun for name plate, name tag, or label. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"名札は付けてたの？","en":"Were you wearing a name tag?","furigana":"なふだはつけてたの？"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"名","radical":"口","meaning":"name","reading":"な"},{"kanji":"札","radical":"木","meaning":"tag","reading":"ふだ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c2', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"ナプキン","reading":"ナプキン","meaning":"(table) napkin or serviette.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture ナプキン wearing a name tag that says ''napukin'', proudly announcing (table) napkin or serviette like it just won a tiny game show.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":null,"pitchAccent":null,"nuance":"Use ナプキン as the basic noun for (table) napkin or serviette. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"ナプキンがありません。","en":"There is a napkin missing.","furigana":"ナプキンがありません。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c3', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"生臭い","reading":"なまぐさい","meaning":"Smelling of fish, fishy, or smelling of blood.","partOfSpeech":"い adjective","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Imagine 生臭い as a button labeled ''namagusai''; press it and the room instantly demonstrates smelling of fish, fishy, or smelling of blood with too much confidence.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":4,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 生臭い to describe something as smelling of fish, fishy, or smelling of blood. It attaches to simple descriptions and noun phrases, so it is most useful when you want to state a visible quality, condition, feeling, or character.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"まな板が生臭いです。","en":"The chopping board smells fishy.","furigana":"まないたがなまぐさいです。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"生","radical":"生","meaning":"life","reading":"いきる"},{"kanji":"臭","radical":"自","meaning":"stinking","reading":"くさい"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c4', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"生ぬるい","reading":"なまぬるい","meaning":"Lukewarm or tepid.","partOfSpeech":"い adjective","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Think of 生ぬるい shouting ''namanurui'' from a billboard whenever lukewarm or tepid appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":4,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 生ぬるい to describe something as lukewarm or tepid. It attaches to simple descriptions and noun phrases, so it is most useful when you want to state a visible quality, condition, feeling, or character.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"水は、生ぬるかった。","en":"The water was lukewarm.","furigana":"みずは、なまぬるかった。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"生","radical":"生","meaning":"life","reading":"なま"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c5', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"生身","reading":"なまみ","meaning":"Living flesh, flesh and blood, or living (human).","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"See 生身 on a sticky note stuck to living flesh, flesh and blood, or living (human); the note keeps saying ''namami'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 生身 as the basic noun for living flesh, flesh and blood, or living (human). It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"これは生身です。","en":"This is living flesh, flesh and blood, or living (human).","furigana":"これはなまみです。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"生","radical":"生","meaning":"life","reading":"いきる"},{"kanji":"身","radical":"身","meaning":"somebody","reading":"み"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c6', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"鉛","reading":"なまり","meaning":"Lead (Pb).","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 鉛 and saying ''namari'' while acting out lead (Pb) with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 鉛 as the basic noun for lead (Pb). It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"鉛は金属である。","en":"Lead is metal.","furigana":"なまりはきんぞくである。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"鉛","radical":"金","meaning":"lead","reading":"なまり"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c7', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"並","reading":"なみ","meaning":"Average, medium, or common.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture 並 wearing a name tag that says ''nami'', proudly announcing average, medium, or common like it just won a tiny game show.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 並 as the basic noun for average, medium, or common. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"波が高い。","en":"The waves are high.","furigana":"なみがたかい。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"並","radical":"一","meaning":"row","reading":"なみ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c8', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"滑らか","reading":"なめらか","meaning":"Smooth (of a surface), glassy, or velvety.","partOfSpeech":"な adjective","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Imagine 滑らか as a button labeled ''nameraka''; press it and the room instantly demonstrates smooth (of a surface), glassy, or velvety with too much confidence.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":2,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 滑らか to describe something as smooth (of a surface), glassy, or velvety. It attaches to simple descriptions and noun phrases, so it is most useful when you want to state a visible quality, condition, feeling, or character.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"フランス語は滑らかには話せません。","en":"I don''t speak French fluently.","furigana":"フランスごはなめらかにははなせません。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"滑","radical":"水","meaning":"slippery","reading":"なめ"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007c9', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"舐める","reading":"なめる","meaning":"To lick, lap, or suck.","partOfSpeech":"る verb","frequencyRank":3715,"mnemonic":"Think of 舐める shouting ''nameru'' from a billboard whenever lick, lap, or suck appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":2,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 舐める for the action or change of lick, lap, or suck. The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"俺をなめるな。","en":"Don''t make light of me.","furigana":"おれをなめるな。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"舐","radical":"舌","meaning":"lick","reading":"な"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007ca', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"悩ましい","reading":"なやましい","meaning":"Seductive, carnal, or enchanting.","partOfSpeech":"い adjective","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"See 悩ましい on a sticky note stuck to seductive, carnal, or enchanting; the note keeps saying ''nayamashii'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":4,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 悩ましい to describe something as seductive, carnal, or enchanting. It attaches to simple descriptions and noun phrases, so it is most useful when you want to state a visible quality, condition, feeling, or character.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"彼女は悩ましい目つきで僕を見た。","en":"She looked at me seductively.","furigana":"かのじょはなやましいめつきでぼくをみた。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"悩","radical":"心","meaning":"trouble","reading":"なや"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007cb', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"悩ます","reading":"なやます","meaning":"To afflict, torment, or harass.","partOfSpeech":"う verb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 悩ます and saying ''nayamasu'' while acting out afflict, torment, or harass with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 悩ます for the action or change of afflict, torment, or harass. The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"五十肩に悩まされています。","en":"I''m suffering from a frozen shoulder.","furigana":"ごじゅうがたになやまされています。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"悩","radical":"心","meaning":"trouble","reading":"なや"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007cc', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"悩み","reading":"なやみ","meaning":"Trouble, troubles, or worry.","partOfSpeech":"Noun","frequencyRank":2258,"mnemonic":"Picture 悩み wearing a name tag that says ''nayami'', proudly announcing trouble, troubles, or worry like it just won a tiny game show.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 悩み as the basic noun for trouble, troubles, or worry. It names a person, thing, place, time, or idea directly, and it commonly appears with particles or です in simple sentences.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"深刻な悩みがあるの。","en":"I have a serious problem.","furigana":"しんこくななやみがあるの。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"悩","radical":"心","meaning":"trouble","reading":"なや"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007cd', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"慣らす","reading":"ならす","meaning":"To accustom or train (e.g. one''s ear).","partOfSpeech":"う verb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Imagine 慣らす as a button labeled ''narasu''; press it and the room instantly demonstrates accustom or train (e.g. one''s ear) with too much confidence.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":2,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 慣らす for the action or change of accustom or train (e.g. one''s ear). The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"新しい靴を履き慣らしている。","en":"I''m breaking in my new shoes.","furigana":"あたらしいくつをはきならしている。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"慣","radical":"心","meaning":"accustomed","reading":"な"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007ce', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"並びに","reading":"ならびに","meaning":"And (also), both ... and, or as well as.","partOfSpeech":"Conjunction","frequencyRank":3461,"mnemonic":"Think of 並びに shouting ''narabini'' from a billboard whenever and (also), both ... and, or as well as appears, as if vocabulary needed dramatic advertising.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":null,"pitchAccent":null,"nuance":"Use 並びに as a set expression in common conversation. It carries its meaning as a whole phrase, so it is best learned with short examples and the situation where it naturally appears.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"並びに、行きます。","en":"And (also), both ... and, or as well as.","furigana":"ならびに、いきます。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"並","radical":"一","meaning":"row","reading":"なら"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007cf', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"成り立つ","reading":"なりたつ","meaning":"To consist of, be made up of, or be composed of.","partOfSpeech":"う verb","frequencyRank":2388,"mnemonic":"See 成り立つ on a sticky note stuck to consist of, be made up of, or be composed of; the note keeps saying ''naritatsu'' until the sound refuses to leave.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":3,"pitchAccent":"nakadaka","nuance":"Use 成り立つ for the action or change of consist of, be made up of, or be composed of. The dictionary form is useful before grammar like こと, while polite sentences usually change the ending. Focus on the core action shown in the example.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"社会は個人から成り立っている。","en":"The community is made up of individuals.","furigana":"しゃかいはこじんからなりたっている。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"成","radical":"戈","meaning":"turn into","reading":"な"},{"kanji":"立","radical":"立","meaning":"stand up","reading":"た"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('a0000001-0000-4000-8000-0000000007d0', '11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000001', 'vocabulary', 'N1', '{"word":"成る丈","reading":"なるたけ","meaning":"As (much) as possible, as (much) as one can, or wherever practicable.","partOfSpeech":"Adverb","frequencyRank":null,"mnemonic":"Picture a tiny teacher pointing at 成る丈 and saying ''narutake'' while acting out as (much) as possible, as (much) as one can, or wherever practicable with ridiculous seriousness.","picture":null,"pitchPosition":0,"pitchAccent":"heiban","nuance":"Use 成る丈 to adjust how an action, state, or whole sentence feels. It usually sits near the verb or adjective it modifies and adds timing, degree, manner, or speaker attitude.","exampleSentences":[{"ja":"成る丈使います。","en":"I use it as (much) as possible, as (much) as one can, or wherever practicable.","furigana":"なるたけつかいます。"}],"kanjiBreakdown":[{"kanji":"成","radical":"戈","meaning":"turn into","reading":"な"},{"kanji":"丈","radical":"一","meaning":"length","reading":"たけ"}]}'::jsonb)
